y, a strong environment, and a dominant money advantage, while most expert raters call it Likely or Lean D and markets price her near 90%. Sparse and stale polling and incoming outside spending argue for keeping the margin near six points rather than higher.", "watch": "Sustained Trump-aligned outside spending or a meaningful loss of Democratic-primary voters to third-party alternatives could pull this into tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 75 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Marie Gluesenkamp Perez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Democrat Marie Gluesenkamp Perez faces Republican state Senator John Braun in one of Washington's last swing districts, which leans slightly Republican. Perez holds a large fundraising advantage and enters with incumbency benefits, while Braun has raised a solid but smaller sum and outside groups have begun spending on both sides. Expert raters split between Likely D, Lean D, and Tossup, and prediction markets price Perez around 90 percent to win.\n\nPolling is thin and dated, with only one recent live-caller survey showing Perez ahead, so the forecast leans on fundamentals and ratings. The race is competitive enough that a strong Republican closing argument or heavy outside investment could narrow it, but the evidence currently favors the incumbent by a moderate margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8833, "margin": 7.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8554, "margin": 6.0}]}